## A union is a set of possibilities

`A | B` is a value that is either an `A` or a `B`. The members can be any types — primitives, objects, literals:

```ts
type Id = string | number;
type Size = 'sm' | 'md' | 'lg'; // literal union
type Result = Success | Failure;
```

## You can only use the common surface

Until you narrow, the compiler allows only members that exist on every branch:

```ts
function format(id: string | number) {
  id.toUpperCase();      // Error — number has no toUpperCase
  return id.toString();  // fine — both have toString
}
```

## Narrowing

A `typeof`, `instanceof`, `in`, or equality check tells the compiler which branch you are in:

```ts
function format(id: string | number) {
  if (typeof id === 'string') return id.toUpperCase(); // id: string here
  return id.toFixed(2);                                // id: number here
}
```

## Discriminated unions

Give each member a shared literal tag and a switch over it narrows cleanly and exhaustively:

```ts
type State =
  | { status: 'loading' }
  | { status: 'success'; data: string }
  | { status: 'error'; message: string };

function render(s: State) {
  switch (s.status) {
    case 'loading': return 'Loading...';
    case 'success': return s.data;     // data is in scope
    case 'error':   return s.message;
  }
}
```

This is the idiomatic way to model component state, API results, and reducer actions — far safer than a bag of optional fields.
